        sec('AI in the results', 'How do AI Overviews work?', ['How do AI Overviews work? Google’s Gemini models read pages from Google’s own index and write a short summary above the results, with links to the pages they drew on. AI Mode does the same in a longer, conversational form.', 'They do not replace the three stages. They sit on top of them, so a page that was never crawled or indexed cannot be quoted in the summary.'],
            more=('AI Overview optimization', '/services/ai-overview-optimization/'), eid='overviews'),
        sec('Assistants', 'How does ChatGPT search work?', ['How does ChatGPT search work? When a question needs current information, ChatGPT searches the web, reads a handful of pages and writes an answer that cites them. It also has its own crawler, OAI-SearchBot, which sites can allow or block by name.', 'Other assistants follow the same pattern with different sources. The reading is new; the need to be readable, confirmed and chosen is not.'],
            more=('How to get my business recommended by ChatGPT', '/problems/not-recommended-by-chatgpt/'), eid='chatgpt'),
        sec('One model', 'What do all of these have in common?', ['Each of them takes a person’s question and names the most relevant answer it can trust. That is the first observation behind the D’Souza Equation: Relevance = Read × Confirm × Choose.', 'Crawling and indexing are Read. Agreement from other sources is Confirm. What people click, call and return to is Choose.'],
            terms([('Stages 1 and 2', 'gold', 'Read', 'Crawling and indexing: can the engine fetch the page and understand it?', 'On page SEO', '/dsouza-equation/read/'),
                   ('Stage 3', 'green', 'Confirm', 'Serving: do other sources say the same thing about the business?', 'Off page SEO', '/dsouza-equation/confirm/'),
                   ('Stage 3', 'green', 'Choose', 'Serving: when shown the business, do people pick it?', 'Conversion rate optimization', '/dsouza-equation/choose/')]),
            eid='model'),
        sec('Check yourself', 'How do I check where my site stops?', ['Three checks, one for each stage, in about ten minutes. They show which stage your business is stuck at.'],
            steps([('Step 1', 'Crawling', 'Open yoursite.com/robots.txt. A line reading “Disallow: /” shuts every crawler out.'),
                   ('Step 2', 'Indexing', 'Search site: followed by your domain. Compare the count with the pages you published.'),
                   ('Step 3', 'Serving', 'Search your trade and city in a private window. See whether you are on the map, in the AI answer, or on page one.')], 3),
            T('<p>For example, an HVAC company in Dallas might pass steps 1 and 2 and still be missing at step 3 for “ac repair dallas”. Then the site is read, and the problem is confirmation or choice.</p>', 'small rs-note', mt=22, mb=18),
            B('Why is my business not showing up on Google?', '/problems/not-showing-up-on-google/', 'rs-more', mb=0), eid='check'),
        sec('What we do', 'How does this shape the work we do?', ['The 413 checks of the D’Souza Standard follow the same path. The technical checks cover crawling and indexing, the listing and review checks cover confirmation, and the conversion checks cover choice.', 'They are run on day three of every plan, and the biggest fixes go live on day five.'],
            more=('Our SEO strategy, day by day', '/dsouza-equation/method/'), eid='work'),
        faq('Questions about how search works', 'What do people ask about how Google search works?', 'Short answers. For anything else, use the <a href="/ask/">Ask page</a>.', [
            ('How Google search works, in one sentence?', 'It finds pages, works out what each one is about, and for every search shows the pages it judges most useful to that person, in that place, at that moment.\nEverything else, from links to reviews to AI summaries, is detail inside those three steps.'),
            ('How does Google search work so fast?', 'Because the hard work is done before you search. Google has already crawled and indexed the pages, so a search is a lookup in a prepared index, not a fresh read of the web.\nThat is also why a new page cannot appear until Google has crawled it.'),
            ('How does the Google search engine work for a local search?', 'For a local search Google adds place to the picture. It says local results turn mainly on relevance, distance and prominence, and it draws on Business Profiles as well as websites.\nThat is why a complete, accurate profile matters as much as the site.'),
            ('Does Google search work the same for everyone?', 'No. Results change with where you are, what you searched before and the device in your hand. Two people can type the same words and see different businesses.\nA private window, on a phone, inside your service area gives the fairest test.'),
            ('Does an AI answer use the same pages as search?', 'Largely, yes. Google’s AI answers are written from pages in Google’s index, and assistants such as ChatGPT search the web and cite what they read.\nA page that search engines cannot read is not available to quote in an AI answer either.'),
        ]),
